      q: 'Describe how the model of the atom developed from the plum pudding model to the modern nuclear model, referring to the key experimental evidence.',
      marks: 12, command: 'Describe',
      modelAnswer: 'Before the discovery of the electron, atoms were thought to be tiny spheres that could not be divided. When Thomson discovered the electron, the plum pudding model was proposed, in which the atom was a ball of positive charge with negative electrons embedded in it. In the alpha particle scattering experiment, Geiger and Marsden, working under Rutherford, fired positively charged alpha particles at thin gold foil. Most passed straight through, showing that the atom is mostly empty space, but some were deflected through large angles and a very few bounced back. This could only be explained if the mass and positive charge were concentrated in a tiny central nucleus, so the nuclear model replaced the plum pudding model. Niels Bohr then adapted the nuclear model by suggesting that electrons orbit the nucleus at specific distances in shells, and his theoretical calculations agreed with experimental observations. Later experiments showed that the positive charge of the nucleus could be subdivided into smaller particles called protons, and about twenty years after the nucleus became accepted, James Chadwick provided evidence for neutrons. The model therefore changed step by step as new evidence became available.'
